    {
      "question": "e-467",
      "field": null,
      "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
      "old_sha256": "789fda5bbae00d936463a7c33d1379b22d90c3ad0bc398f76e4f372a49dda0ee",
      "new_sha256": "b47c172f374832ab8de85f7cf2ba18851cf2d4fb672031b49c3d8596769eae7f",
      "category": "ma_capture",
      "authoring": [],
      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-467",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "Notice that the numerator is proportional to the derivative of the denominator, because\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}(1+\\tan^2 x) = 2\\tan{x}\\sec^2 x.\n$$\n\nSo, let $u = 1+\\tan^2 x.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d} u}{\\textrm{d}x } = 2\\tan x\\sec^2 x \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 1 2 \\textrm d u = \\tan x\\sec^2 x\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\pi/4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x{sec}^{2}\u2061x}{1+{tan}^{2}\u2061x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{1+{tan}^{2}\u2061x}\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}\u2061x{sec}^{2}\u2061x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}\u20612-\\operatorname{ln}\u20611) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}\u20612\\end{aligned}\n$$",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006406/example-467.json",
          "file_sha256": "db02471074f16fa8b9e64424e3342ec9aa1da5c30b0a5fe6a5aef89adcb9cf93"
        }
